Environmental law provides the legal structure to protect our natural environment in a variety of forms. This includes the protection of air, land and water by addressing pollution (affecting both terrestrial and aquatic life forms including people), as well as conservation of the environment (i.e., protection of habitat). Other forms are more specific like legislation for protecting individual and commercial wildlife, natural resources (i.e., water, timber, oil, gas etc.), plant and animal life and even the protection of landscapes (i.e., scenic landscapes or places that are culturally valuable, have significant history or religious value and which are worth preserving irrespective of any practical significance).
